==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Translation of the Latin term {{m|la|dominium}} {{m|la|ēminēns}} “supreme ownership”.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|legal|US|Philippines}} The right of a [[government]] over the private property within its [[jurisdiction]]. Usually [[invoke]]d to compel land owners to sell their property in preparation for a major construction project such as a [[freeway]].
#: ''The city council used '''eminent domain''' to make me sell my store.''
# {{lb|en|historical}} In [[feudalism]], the legal interest or rights of a [[lord]] or [[superior]] in an [[estate in land]] held in [[fee]], as opposed to the vassal's or tenant's interest.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|compulsory purchase}} {{q|UK|Ireland|New Zealand}}, [[compulsory acquisition]] {{q|Australia}}, [[expropriation]] {{q|Canada|South Africa}}
* [[superiority]] {{qualifier|Scots law}}

====Antonyms====
* {{lb|en|feudal law}}: [[utile domain]], [[inferiority]] (Scots law)

